```suggestion
    ValueTask IAsyncDisposable.DisposeAsync()
    {
        NavigationManager.RemoveLocationChangingHandler(OnLocationChanging);
        return JSRuntime.InvokeVoidAsync(NavigationLockInterop.DisableNavigationPrompt, _id);
    }
```

@javiercn The challenge with having the `CancellationToken` as its own argument in the callback is that `EventCallback<TValue>` only allows there to be a single parameter, so if you define a location changing handler using `<NavigationLock OnBeforeInternalNavigation="Foo" />`, then `Foo()` can only accept a single argument (the `LocationChangingContext`). So the only option in that case is to leave the `CancellationToken` as a property on `LocationChangingContext`.

I guess one way we could make this work is that we have a separate `LocationChangingEventArgs` whose two properties are the `LocationChangingContext` and the `CancellationToken`. So, if you add a location changing handler using the `NavigationManager` API, your callback will still have a separate argument for the `CancellationToken`. I'm not sure how I feel about having to do `args.Context.PreventNavigation()` rather than just `context.PreventNavigation()` though.

Thoughts?

You can do this with a TaskCompletionSource too.

```
var tcs = new TaskCompletionSource();
ct.Register(() => tcs.SetCanceled());
```

and inside this method you do await tcs.Task;
